{"graph_chars": "Households, institutions, and businesses in the United States saved almost one point three trillion dollars in two thousand fifteen. Savers supply financial capital. Borrowers demand it. The price is a rate of return. The simplest rate of return is the interest rate. In two thousand twenty-one, almost two hundred million Americans held credit cards. Typical card rates twelve to eighteen percent. May two thousand twenty-one: about eight hundred seven billion dollars of card debt outstanding. Just over forty-five percent of families carried some. The book's market: average fifteen percent a year. Figure four point five and Table four point five. Equilibrium fifteen percent and six hundred billion dollars loaned. At twenty-one percent, supply seven hundred fifty billion, demand four hundred eighty: a surplus of funds, and firms cut rates. At thirteen percent, demand seven hundred billion, supply five hundred ten: a shortage, and firms raise rates. Law of demand still holds: higher interest, less borrowing. Law of supply: higher interest, more lending. FRED publishes two dozen interest-rate series — cards, cars, mortgages.", "graph_times": [[0.0, 0.0632], [0.0632, 0.1263], [0.1263, 0.1895], [0.1895, 0.2526], [0.2526, 0.3158], [0.3158, 0.3789], [0.3789, 0.4421], [0.4421, 0.5052], [0.5052, 0.5684], [0.5684, 0.6315], [0.6315, 0.6947], [0.6947, 0.7578], [0.7578, 0.821], [0.821, 0.8841], [0.8841, 0.9473], [0.9473, 1.0104], [1.0104, 1.0736], [1.0736, 1.1367], [1.1367, 1.1999], [1.1999, 1.263], [1.263, 1.3262], [1.3262, 1.3893], [1.3893, 1.4525], [1.4525, 1.5156], [1.5156, 1.5788], [1.5788, 1.6419], [1.6419, 1.7051], [1.7051, 1.7682], [1.7682, 1.8314], [1.8314, 1.8945], [1.8945, 1.9577], [1.9577, 2.0208], [2.0208, 2.084], [2.084, 2.1471], [2.1471, 2.2103], [2.2103, 2.2735], [2.2735, 2.3366], [2.3366, 2.3998], [2.3998, 2.4629], [2.4629, 2.5261], [2.5261, 2.5892], [2.5892, 2.6524], [2.6524, 2.7155], [2.7155, 2.7787], [2.7787, 2.8418], [2.8418, 2.905], [2.905, 2.